\begin_layout Title
General Equilibrium: Homework 1 (2012)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a pure exchange economy with indivisibilities in consumption.
 Suppose that there are 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 traders (indexed by subscripts 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

) and each trader 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has a consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=\mathbb{N}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{il}$
\end_inset

 is an integer for each 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,l$
\end_inset

, an intial endowment vector 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\in X_{i}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\gg0$
\end_inset

, and preferences 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

, which are asusmed to be complete preorders on 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\ \forall\ i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Give some examples of commodities that are likely to be available or consumed
 in integer amounts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
True or false and explain why: For this ecnomy, the asusmption that preferences
 are continuous is innocuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Define monotonicity and strict monotonicity of preferences for this economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Can you define convexity of preferences for this economy? Either state a
 definition or explain why a definition cannot be given.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is it reasonable to assume, in this economy, that preferences are locally
 non-satiated?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Does such an economy (assuming that preferences satisfy as many of the above
 properties as possible) necessarily have a competitive equilibrium? Explain
 why or why not?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
