\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\equiv\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
 To calculate the demand function of an agent, we must maximize his utility
 function subject to his budget constraint.
 In this situation, we must consider the boundary cases when one of the
 prices equals zero, as well as the interior case when both prices are positive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}>0,p_{2}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{1}(x_{11},x_{12})=\min\left\{ 2x_{11},3(x_{12}-3)\right\} \\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{11}+px_{12}=2+2p
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

From the budget constraint, we have an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{11}=2+2p-px_{12}
\]

\end_inset

which we plug into the utility function and solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{12}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
4+4p-2px_{12} & = & 3x_{12}-9\\
(2p+3)x_{12} & = & 13+4p\\
x_{12} & = & \frac{13+4p}{2p+3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Finally, we solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2x_{11} & = & \frac{39+12p}{2p+3}-\left(\frac{18p+27}{2p+3}\right)\\
2x_{11} & = & \frac{12-6p}{2p+3}\\
x_{11} & = & \frac{6-3p}{2p+3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}>0,p_{2}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the budget constraint, we get 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}=2$
\end_inset

.
 Plugging this amount for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}$
\end_inset

 into the utility funciton we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
4 & = & 3x_{12}-9\\
x_{12} & = & \frac{13}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, agent 1 can only purchase 2 units of good 1, and to maximize his utility,
 he will demand at least 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{13}{3}$
\end_inset

 units of good 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}=0,p_{2}>0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

