\begin_layout Problem
Consider a pure exchange economy with 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $L=3$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose the consumers have utility functions given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
u_{1}(x_{11},x_{12},x_{13})=\min\left\{ x_{11},x_{12},x_{13}\right\} ;\ w_{1}=\left(1,1,1\right)\\
u_{2}(x_{21},x_{22},x_{23})=\left(x_{21}\cdot x_{22}\cdot x_{23}\right)^{\nicefrac{1}{3}};\ w_{2}=\left(1,1,1\right)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive aggregate excess demand functions for the three commodities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find a competitive equilibrium allocation and price, and prove that it is
 unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is the equilibrium you found in part b globally stable? Why?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\equiv\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\equiv\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
 To derive the aggregate excess demand functions, we must first calculate
 the demand function of both agents by maximizing each one's utility function
 subject to his budget constraint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The maximization problem for agent 1 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\max\ u_{1}(x_{11},x_{12},x_{13})=\min\left\{ x_{11},x_{12},x_{13}\right\} \\
\mbox{s.t.}\ \ \ x_{11}+px_{12}+p^{\prime}x_{13}=1+p+p^{\prime}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

From the utility function and the budget constraint, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}=x_{12}=x_{13}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For agent 2, any monotonic transformation of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

 still represents his preferences.
 So, we will consider the function 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{2}(\cdot))^{3}$
\end_inset

.
 The maximization problem for agent 2 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\max\ u_{2}(x_{21},x_{22},x_{23})=x_{21}\cdot x_{22}\cdot x_{23}\\
\mbox{s.t.}\ \ \ x_{21}+px_{22}+p^{\prime}x_{23}=1+p+p^{\prime}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The Lagrangian is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=x_{21}\cdot x_{22}\cdot x_{23}+\lambda(1+p+p^{\prime}-x_{21}-px_{22}-p^{\prime}x_{23})
\]

\end_inset

After calculating partial derivatives, we have the following first-order
 conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{21}}=0 & \iff & x_{22}x_{23}=\lambda\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{22}}=0 & \iff & \frac{x_{21}x_{23}}{p}=\lambda\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{23}}=0 & \iff & \frac{x_{21}x_{22}}{p^{\prime}}=\lambda
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Setting these equations equal to one another, we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x_{22}x_{23}=\frac{x_{21}x_{23}}{p}=\frac{x_{21}x_{22}}{p^{\prime}}\\
\frac{1}{x_{21}}=\frac{1}{px_{22}}=\frac{1}{p^{\prime}x_{23}}\\
x_{21}=px_{22}=p^{\prime}x_{23}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

From the equation above and the budget constraint, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{21}+p\left(\frac{x_{21}}{p}\right)+p^{\prime}\left(\frac{x_{21}}{p^{\prime}}\right) & = & 1+p+p^{\prime}\\
x_{21} & = & \frac{1}{3}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right)\\
x_{22} & = & \frac{1}{3p}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right)\\
x_{23} & = & \frac{1}{3p^{\prime}}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the aggregate demand functions for the three commodities are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\hat{z}_{1}=\hat{x}_{1}-\hat{w}_{1}=\frac{1}{3}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right)-1\\
\hat{z}_{2}=\hat{x}_{2}-\hat{w}_{2}=\frac{1}{3p}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right)-1\\
\hat{z}_{3}=\hat{x}_{3}-\hat{w}_{3}=\frac{1}{3p^{\prime}}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right)-1
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the competitive equilibrium, we must find all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}=0$
\end_inset

.
 By Walras' Law, it is enough to show only two markets clearing for 
\begin_inset Formula $L=3$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider the markets for good 1 and 2, respectively.
 By the market clearing conditions, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{3}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right) & = & 1\\
p & = & 2-p^{\prime}\\
\frac{1}{3p}\left(1+p+p^{\prime}\right) & = & 1\\
p & = & \frac{1+p^{\prime}}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Setting the two equations for 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 equal to one another we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2-p^{\prime} & = & \frac{1+p^{\prime}}{2}\\
4-2p^{\prime} & = & 1+p^{\prime}\\
p^{\prime} & = & 1\\
p & = & 1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, a competitive equilibrium is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*},p^{*}\right]=\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

From the aggregate demand functions, it is evident that if 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{l}=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}^{l}(p^{*})>0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,2,3$
\end_inset

.
 So, all goods are desirable.
 In addition, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\hat{z}^{h}}{\partial p^{l}}>0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $l\neq h$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, all goods are gross substitutes.
 Finally, note that Walras' Law holds.
 Therefore, by Theorem 7.5.1, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a unique competitive equilibrium price vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since Walras' Law holds and all goods are desirable and gross substitutes
 for all prices, then by Theorem 7.6.3, the competitive equilibrium is globally
 stable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider an economy with two firms and two consumers.
 Firm 1 is entirely owned by consumer 1.
 It produces guns form oil via the production function 
\begin_inset Formula $g=2x$
\end_inset

.
 Firm 2 is entirely owned by consumer 2; it produces butter form oil via
 production function 
\begin_inset Formula $b=3x$
\end_inset

.
 Each consumer owns 10 units of oil.
 Consumer 1's utility function is 
\begin_inset Formula $u(g,b)=g^{0.4}b^{0.6}$
\end_inset

 and consumer 2's utility function is 
\begin_inset Formula $u(g,b)=10+0.5\ln g+0.5\ln b$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the market clearing prices for guns, butter, and oil.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
How many guns and how much butter does each consumer consume?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
How much oil does each firm use?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, let us normalize the price of oil to 1.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{g}\equiv\frac{p_{g}}{p_{o}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{b}\equiv\frac{p_{b}}{p_{o}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since both firms exhibit constant returns to scale, then the profit for
 each firm will be zero in equilibrium.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{g}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{b}=\frac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the market clearing prices for guns, butter, and oil are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(p_{o}^{*},p_{g}^{*},p_{b}^{*}\right)^{\prime}=\left(1,\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{3}\right)^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find out how much of each commodity the agents consume, we must derive
 their demand functions.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
 Since both agents have Cobb-Douglas preferences, then their demand functions
 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1}(p) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{4}{p_{g}}\\
\frac{6}{p_{b}}
\end{array}\right)\\
x_{2}(p) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{5}{p_{g}}\\
\frac{5}{p_{b}}
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, given the prices found in part a, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{1}=8$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}=18$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{2}=10$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{2}=15$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the market clear conditions, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{Firm 1}:\ 8+10 & = & 2x\\
x & = & 9\\
\mbox{Firm 2:}\ 18+15 & = & 3x\\
x & = & 11
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the total amount of oil used is 20, which is equal to the total units
 available in the economy.
\end_layout

